// Practice quiz: Path, query and body parameters
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/path-query-and-body-parameters

::o1 optional query::How do you make a query parameter optional? {
=Set required \= false or give a defaultValue#Correct. Otherwise it is required.
~Use @PathVariable#Not quite. That binds a path segment and is required by the mapping.
~Declare the parameter private#Not quite. Parameters have no access modifiers.
~Put it in the body#Not quite. That changes where the value is read from.
}

::o1 body binding::Which annotation turns a JSON request body into a Java object? {
=@RequestBody#Correct. It uses a message converter to read the body.
~@RequestParam#Not quite. That reads the query string or form values.
~@PathVariable#Not quite. That reads the URL path.
~@ResponseBody#Not quite. That concerns the response.
}

::o2 conversion failure::A path variable is declared long and the URL segment is abc. What status results? {
=400#Correct. A conversion failure is a client error.
~200#Not quite. The handler does not run.
~404#Not quite. The path pattern still matched.
~500#Not quite. The server treats it as a bad request.
}

::o2 missing required::A required query parameter is absent from the request. What status results? {
=400#Correct. Missing required parameters are client errors.
~200#Not quite. The handler does not run.
~204#Not quite. That is a success status.
~405#Not quite. That means the HTTP method is not allowed.
}

::o3 choosing placement::Where does the sort order for a listing endpoint usually belong? {
=The query string#Correct. It refines what the listing returns.
~The path, as an extra resource#Not quite. Sort order does not identify a resource.
~A JSON body on GET#Not quite. GET does not normally carry a meaningful body.
~A database column#Not quite. That is storage, not a request value.
}

